PART A — STEP-IN (ATTACK LUNGE)

2. Universal rule: every ordinary grounded melee attack must Step-in

Every grounded melee attack must automatically make its fighter advance slightly forward during the attack animation.

This applies to:

* Basic punches
* Basic kicks
* Swipes
* Claw attacks
* Grounded melee combinations
* Grounded multi-hit melee attacks
* Grounded launchers and finishers
* Grounded close-range physical attacks
* Grounded melee attacks regardless of their current mobility tier
* Future grounded melee attacks added to the roster

The Step-in must not depend on a movement input.

A player who stands still and presses a grounded melee attack must still advance slightly forward.

The Step-in is an intentional part of the attack, not an ordinary run, Dash, or change of position caused by sprite animation.

Required behavior

* Apply the Step-in in the fighter’s current attack-facing direction.
* Use a small, controlled advance consistent with the attack’s animation.
* Trigger it once per attack activation, at an appropriate point in the attack’s startup or strike.
* Preserve the attack’s startup, active, and recovery timings.
* Do not change hitbox damage, launch, hitstun, cooldown, or cancel behavior.
* Never teleport or snap the fighter forward.
* Respect collision, walls, stage geometry, and ordinary movement physics.
* Do not push through a wall or create an unintended stage bypass.
* Do not reapply the Step-in each animation frame.
* Do not continuously accelerate the fighter throughout the entire attack.

An existing, intentionally authored Step-in should continue behaving as authored instead of receiving an additional default Step-in.

One attack activation must not receive two lunges because it qualifies for both an authored Step-in and the universal default.

3. Reuse Alva’s existing Step-in implementation

Alva already supports:

step: { at, speed }

The shared steerAttack() function in js/game/fighters/movement.js currently checks when attack time reaches step.at and raises the fighter’s forward velocity to at least the configured speed while grounded.

Build on this mechanism instead of inventing a separate physics system.

However, the universal rule cannot depend solely on character authors manually supplying a step property.

Introduce a shared, consistently resolved default for eligible grounded melee attacks.

Possible implementation approaches include:

* Resolving an effective Step-in policy when the attack definition is created.
* Computing a default Step-in from the resolved attack classification and timing.
* A small, well-defined shared policy module if it makes ownership clearer.

Choose the smallest architecture-consistent solution.

The implementation must support:

* A sensible shared default Step-in timing.
* A sensible shared default Step-in strength.
* An attack’s explicitly authored timing and strength taking precedence.
* Deliberate move-specific exceptions.
* Consistent behavior across all fighters.

The advance must remain slight. It should improve grounded melee connection and visual commitment without turning ordinary attacks into unintended dashes or homing attacks.

Do not simply apply an arbitrary high velocity to every melee move.

If different timing is justified for single-hit and multi-hit attacks, derive it from their real strike windows and existing authored animation timing.

4. Aerial melee attacks must NOT receive automatic Step-in

Aerial melee attacks must never acquire a Step-in solely because of this universal update.

Examples:

* Midair punches
* Midair kicks
* Air swipes
* Midair melee combinations
* Falling melee attacks
* Air launchers
* Air finishers

Do not automatically add or inherit the grounded melee Step-in for:

* midair_attack1
* midair_attack2
* midair_attack3
* Other aerial attacks
* Any attack activated while airborne

This must be based on the actual attack context and mechanics, not only a filename or naming convention.

Critical exception: preserve actual move mechanics

Some aerial attacks have deliberately scripted movement intrinsic to the move, such as:

* Homing dashes
* Rising attacks
* Falling plunges
* Bouncing attacks
* Slams
* Hovering attacks
* Authored aerial lunges or propulsion

Do not remove, disable, rewrite, weaken, or reinterpret such movement merely because the move is aerial.

The prohibition is against the new automatic/default Step-in, not against an aerial move’s own legitimate movement mechanic.

For example, an aerial homing kick must still home toward its target as designed.

An aerial slam must still plunge downward and produce its authored landing behavior.

Do not add an additional generic forward impulse to either.

If an aerial attack has intentionally authored behavior resembling a lunge, preserve that behavior while keeping it distinct from the new grounded-melee default.

Also handle cases where a fighter leaves the ground or lands during an attack. Do not accidentally create a late or repeated automatic lunge because grounded state changed.

