5. Projectiles, summons, clones, and object-based moves must NOT receive automatic Step-in

By default, moves whose offense is delivered through a projectile, spawned object, summoned entity, clone, trap, field, or comparable construct must not receive the universal Step-in.

This includes:

* Projectile throws
* Beams
* Energy shots
* Orbs
* Summoned clones
* Summoned companions
* Spawned attack objects
* Placed traps
* Persistent damage fields
* Object-based techniques
* Object-based special abilities
* Other attacks whose offensive action is primarily executed by a separate spawned entity or effect

The fighter should not automatically advance merely because it casts, releases, places, or summons something.

Critical exception: preserve explicitly authored move mechanics

If a particular projectile, summon, clone, or object-based move intentionally includes its own forward movement as a fundamental part of the move, preserve that movement exactly as designed.

For example, a move may deliberately involve:

* A forward-moving casting pose
* A lunge followed by projectile release
* An advance before placing a trap
* A dash that creates a projectile
* Movement intrinsic to a summoning technique

Do not remove such movement.

Likewise, if a move is intentionally stationary, do not introduce a Step-in.

Hybrid attacks

Explicitly handle hybrid moves that combine:

* A direct melee hitbox and a projectile
* A direct strike and a spawned effect
* A melee attack and an object
* An attack and projectile deflection

Do not assume every hitbox represents a pure melee move, or that every projectile-containing move has no direct melee component.

Classify the move from its actual mechanics.

When a move fundamentally delivers its offense through a construct, the safe default is no automatic Step-in. A deliberate direct-melee lunge can still be preserved through authored behavior.

Clone execution

A clone using an attack definition is not the same as the owning fighter personally executing that attack.

Do not accidentally apply the universal fighter Step-in to clone positions, summon positions, projectile positions, or other spawned entities.

Existing clone movement and placement mechanics must remain intact.

