PART B — ATTACK STEERING (ATTACK MOBILITY)

6. Universal rule: all ordinary moves must allow Attack Steering

Every move must allow Attack Steering by default unless it is a Special ability, has a 3-second or 5-second cooldown, or has a genuine authored movement mechanic requiring different behavior.

Attack Steering means the fighter can respond to held directional movement input while the move animation is playing.

A player should be able to hold left or right and move during an eligible move rather than being unnecessarily rooted until the animation ends.

The movement may be slower than normal running, using the move’s mobility settings.

This includes eligible:

* Grounded melee moves
* Aerial melee moves
* Projectiles
* Projectile-throwing attacks
* Object-based attacks
* Ordinary techniques
* Ordinary summons with meaningful owner startup
* Basic attacks
* Extra attacks
* Other ordinary combat moves

Do not classify every technique or summon as non-steerable simply because of its implementation type.

A move can be a technique, summon, or projectile attack without being a Special or having a 3-second/5-second cooldown.

Use the existing mobility system

Alva already defines:

* mobility
* momentum
* airMomentum
* control
* airControl
* topSpeed
* airTopSpeed
* friction
* lockMovement

Retain the existing quick, medium, heavy, and planted tiers where appropriate.

For moves that allow steering, movement must remain consistent with their resolved mobility behavior.

Do not make every move accelerate to full running speed.

The rule is that eligible moves allow directional control, not that they all move equally fast.

Steering should work during startup, active, and recovery where the move’s mechanics permit it.

7. No ordinary Attack Steering during Special abilities

Moves explicitly classified as Special abilities must not allow the new/default Attack Steering.

Use Alva’s actual Special classification:

isSpecialAbility(def, move)

as determined from the character’s specialAbilities list.

Do not infer Special status merely from:

* An attack button number
* extra_attack
* A dramatic animation
* High damage
* A projectile
* A technique
* A summon
* A projectile’s visual size
* The CPU capability analyzer’s existing “specials” execution category

A move is a Special because its character definition explicitly classifies it that way.

Exception: inherent movement

If a Special’s actual design deliberately allows steering or gives the player meaningful directional control, retain that mechanic.

If a Special’s own mechanic requires the fighter to move automatically, retain that movement too.

However, a Special without an explicit intrinsic steering mechanic must not gain general-purpose Attack Steering from the universal defaults.

Do not erase a designed dash, roll, homing movement, or directional movement effect simply to make the move stationary.

Disabling Attack Steering must disable movement control, not every possible source of movement.

8. No ordinary Attack Steering during 3-second or 5-second cooldown moves

Alva’s cooldown policy already defines:

* Ordinary move: 0 seconds
* Exceptional move: 3 seconds
* Ten-damage move: 5 seconds

Any move with an effective 3-second or 5-second cooldown must not allow default Attack Steering.

This applies regardless of whether the move is visually labeled Special.

For example:

* A non-Special move with a 3-second cooldown must still have default steering disabled.
* A non-Special move with a 5-second cooldown must still have default steering disabled.
* A Special with no cooldown must still have default steering disabled because of its Special classification.
* An ordinary non-Special move with no cooldown should allow steering unless its actual mechanics explicitly prevent it.

Use the resolved effective cooldown, not a guessed duration or simply the presence of a cooldown field in raw character data.

Apply this consistently to attacks, techniques, and summons.

Do not change the existing cooldown policy or when cooldowns begin.

Exception: intrinsic movement control

A cooldown move that intentionally supports steering as part of its actual mechanics must retain it.

A cooldown move with scripted automatic movement must also retain that scripted movement.

But do not accidentally count automatic momentum, knockback, or scripted motion as permission for unrestricted player steering.

9. Preserve move-specific movement mechanics

This is a fundamental requirement.

The new universal rules must not override intentionally authored behavior.

Inspect actual move data, associated character documentation, and real runtime mechanics to distinguish:

1. A move deliberately designed to be steerable.
2. A move deliberately designed to remain planted.
3. A move deliberately designed to move under its own scripted motion.
4. A move simply receiving old default mobility behavior without an intentional design reason.

Only the fourth category should automatically change when it conflicts with the new policy.

In particular:

* Preserve homing-dash movement.
* Preserve roll movement.
* Preserve bounce and slam mechanics.
* Preserve rise and hover mechanics.
* Preserve authored recoil and rebounds.
* Preserve intentionally stationary casts.
* Preserve intentionally mobile casts.
* Preserve intentionally authored mobility overrides.
* Preserve movement tied to hit-confirmed or other real move-specific mechanics.

Do not automatically treat an old planted value as proof that an entire category of moves must remain stationary. Determine whether the restriction is truly part of that individual move’s intended mechanics.

Conversely, do not erase an explicitly designed planted move simply to make every ordinary move steerable.

The universal defaults must be strong, but authored mechanics must remain authoritative.

