PART E — CPU INTELLIGENCE

13. CPUs must obey exactly the same rules as human players

This update must work equally for CPUs.

A CPU must:

* Execute the same automatic grounded melee Step-ins.
* Receive no automatic aerial Step-ins.
* Receive no automatic Step-ins on object-based moves unless deliberately authored.
* Be able to steer during eligible moves.
* Be unable to steer during restricted Specials and 3/5-second cooldown moves unless intentionally supported by the move.
* Respect the same mobility tiers and movement authority.
* Preserve scripted moves.
* Respect collisions and ledges.
* Use normal legal FighterInput snapshots.

Do not create CPU-only movement speeds, stronger Step-ins, extra steering authority, or movement permissions unavailable to human players.

This must work at every CPU difficulty.

Difficulty may affect decision quality, not movement rules.

14. Remove the CPU’s blanket grounded attack-steering suppression

The existing Executor.finish() includes a rule that neutralizes directional input while an ordinary grounded, movement-locked, non-motion attack is active.

Its purpose is to prevent execution from disagreeing with the CPU’s currently unsteered attack predictions.

This blanket suppression cannot remain the general behavior for steerable attacks.

Update the CPU so that:

* It can deliberately hold left or right during a steerable grounded attack.
* It can steer during eligible aerial attacks.
* It can choose to hold no direction when that is tactically preferable.
* It does not send ineffective steering input for genuinely non-steerable moves.
* Its real movement matches the movement used when planning the attack.

Do not simply delete the restriction without updating the predictive system.

That would let CPUs move away from their own predicted hit positions.

15. Update CPU movement prediction

Inspect js/game/ai/cpu-intelligence/prediction.js.

Its current attack simulations call steerAttack() with a neutral movement direction in several paths.

Update the predictive model to account for:

* The new automatic grounded Step-in.
* The absence of default aerial and object-move Step-ins.
* Whether a move permits Attack Steering.
* The steering direction the CPU plans to hold.
* Attack-specific momentum and movement tiers.
* Authored exceptions.
* Scripted motion that owns the fighter’s velocity.

The CPU must simulate the same physics and policy as the actual Fighter.

Prefer reusing shared movement functions rather than implementing approximate duplicate movement rules.

Ensure the prediction for a candidate attack is consistent with the direction the executor will actually hold.

CPU tactical behavior

A CPU should be capable of:

* Advancing slightly during a grounded melee attack via automatic Step-in.
* Steering toward an opponent during an eligible attack to improve connection.
* Holding position to avoid overshooting.
* Steering away when tactically appropriate and legal.
* Controlling aerial drift during eligible aerial attacks.
* Choosing attack direction appropriately after an opponent changes sides.
* Recognizing that a non-steerable Special is a committed action.
* Accounting for the new approach distance of grounded melee attacks.
* Avoiding unsafe movement toward stage edges.

Do not force CPUs to steer on every attack. They must have the same capability as the player and use it intelligently.

16. Update CPU capability analysis and evaluation

Inspect:

* capabilities.js
* prediction.js
* evaluator.js
* executor.js
* brain.js
* Relevant planning and defensive systems

The capability analyzer should know each move’s effective movement behavior.

The evaluator must not use the old attack position or reach assumptions when a Step-in changes the fighter’s trajectory.

Ensure that:

* Step-in distance is factored into actual candidate contact simulation.
* A move with automatic Step-in does not receive additional imaginary reach.
* The CPU considers steering only when the move actually permits it.
* Projectiles and object attacks are not incorrectly treated as melee lunges.
* Moves with deliberately scripted movement remain correctly represented.
* CPU defense can predict the changed grounded melee attack trajectories.
* AI planning and execution remain deterministic.

Review CPU approach and mobility roles if the newly added default Step-ins change role classification. A tiny universal Step-in must not automatically turn every basic punch into a major movement or recovery ability.

Preserve meaningful CPU behavior across Quick Battle and Watch Mode.

